00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:36.520
In August 2021, President Biden surprised
everyone when he told ABC, quote, “We made

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:37.860
a sacred commitment…

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.780
that if… anyone were to invade… our NATO
allies, we would respond.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:46.590
Same with Japan, same with South Korea, same
with Taiwan."

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:51.459
At best, some argued, this was evidence of
Biden’s “declining mental acuity”.

00:03:51.459 --> 00:03:58.810
At worst, it was intentional, and therefore
irresponsible – even dangerous.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:03.390
Others celebrated this long-awaited, ‘momentous’
announcement.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:09.130
But nearly everyone seemed to agree on one
thing: this was a big deal.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.970
Here’s why…

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:16.970
In 1949, the Chinese Nationalist Party, led
by Chiang Kai-shek, lost the Civil War to

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:22.150
the Communists and fled to what we now call
the island of ‘Taiwan’.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:26.130
Despite this loss, the United States continued
to recognize it as the sole government of

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.490
China for the next 30 years.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:33.780
During that time, the two countries signed
a mutual defense treaty, promising to protect

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.060
one another in case of an attack.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:41.060
American troops were even stationed in Taiwan.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:46.300
That treaty ended in 1980 after the U.S. switched
recognition to the People’s Republic on

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.180
the mainland.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:53.850
In its place, Congress passed the Taiwan Relations
Act, which obligates Washington to do two

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:54.850
things:

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:59.580
First, sell the island weapons, quote, “sufficient
[to maintain its] self-defense capability”.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.980
And second, “maintain the capacity… to
resist any… force… that would jeopardize

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:10.850
the security, or social or economic system,
of the people on Taiwan”.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:14.900
In other words, Biden was not strictly correct.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:19.830
The United States does not have the same “sacred
commitment” to defend Taiwan as it does

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.850
with Japan, South Korea, NATO, and others.

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.700
It has the option, of course.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.870
But not the obligation.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:31.990
Still, perhaps he simply misspoke.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:36.569
As many were quick to point out, it wouldn’t
be the first time.

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:40.360
Then the same thing happened just two months
later.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.240
And again in May.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:48.430
Each time, the White House issued a statement
saying nothing had really changed.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:51.760
And each time, they became less and less persuasive.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:58.350
Finally, in September, Biden went a step further
– clarifying that American troops would

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.480
respond.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.100
After four increasingly-specific statements
in a row, no one could write these off with

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.599
a straight face as just mistakes.

00:06:07.599 --> 00:06:13.430
Politicians, analysts, and journalists announced
the death of America's four-decade-long policy

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:18.850
of refusing to say whether or not it would
defend Taiwan called “Strategic Ambiguity”

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:23.479
established by Jimmy Carter and upheld by
every president since.

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:26.780
But… not so fast.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:32.160
When Carter signed the Taiwan Relations Act
in 1979, America didn’t promise to defend

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.830
Taiwan – that much is true.

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:40.020
Less well remembered is that it also didn’t
support Taiwanese independence.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:44.720
It didn’t even, believe it or not, oppose
its unification with China.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.010
In fact, it was widely assumed that it eventually
would.

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:54.229
Washington’s attitude was “this is for
you two to figure out.”

00:06:54.229 --> 00:07:00.599
The only thing it actually opposed were “unilateral
changes to the status quo”.

00:07:00.599 --> 00:07:06.090
In plain English, anything that wasn’t peacefully
agreed to by both sides.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:09.229
Now, this may ring hollow.

00:07:09.229 --> 00:07:14.180
Surely America wasn’t just some neutral,
disinterested third party.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:19.010
But the ultimate test of whether it’s sincerely
committed to the status quo or just using

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:24.840
it to undermine its Communist adversary is:
Does it only oppose China’s attempts to

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:30.560
change the status quo or does it also oppose
Taiwan’s?

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:35.521
The answer is that whenever Taiwan has even
flirted with the idea of independence, America

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:37.820
has sprung into action.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:43.380
Far from cheerleading for that outcome, it
moves to prevent it by occasionally intervening

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:49.210
in the country’s democratic politics to
a degree that would make many Americans uncomfortable.

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:54.000
George Bush, for example, criticized Taiwan’s
President for making relatively minor, symbolic

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.229
moves toward independence in the early 2000s.

00:07:58.229 --> 00:08:02.720
The Bush administration felt this so important
that it defied intense bipartisan outrage

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:05.680
for “taking China’s side”.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:11.060
What’s truly amazing about the status quo
is how long it’s persisted despite being

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:14.430
no one’s preference.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:19.100
Unification is one of China’s oldest and
most important political projects.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:23.889
The people of Taiwan are stuck in diplomatic
limbo – denied the very real benefits of

00:08:23.889 --> 00:08:26.409
international representation.

00:08:26.409 --> 00:08:30.889
And most Americans would prefer this vibrant
democracy receive the full recognition it

00:08:30.889 --> 00:08:32.719
deserves.

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:38.769
But the guiding principle behind American
policy since 1979 is that – deeply unsatisfying

00:08:38.769 --> 00:08:44.360
as it is – the status quo is the least bad
alternative.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:49.370
The way it’s maintained this delicate balance
for 44 years is by creating room for both

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:53.959
China and Taiwan to believe time is on their
side.

00:08:53.959 --> 00:08:59.390
The trick is that no individual Chinese or
Taiwanese leader needs to immediately unify

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:02.080
or declare independence, respectively.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.610
They just can’t afford to lose that future
possibility.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:13.810
On any given day, forcing unification or declaring
independence would be an extremely risky gamble.

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:18.260
Thus, both leaders have an interest in delaying.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:22.519
Put differently, the two sides of the Strait
do much of the work of preserving peace by

00:09:22.519 --> 00:09:26.830
wearing rose-colored glasses, seeing what
they want to see.

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:33.070
Washington’s only job is to paint a picture
abstract enough that each side can simultaneously

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:37.410
interpret it in a favorable, self-interested
way.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:43.290
What makes this simple task so challenging
is that it’s a continual process.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:47.839
Whenever America perceives a change in this
balance – either from Taiwan drifting toward

00:09:47.839 --> 00:09:54.690
independence, or China gaining in strength
– it steps in to re-establish the equilibrium.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:59.440
Reagan spelled out a version of this principle
in a 1982 memo, saying “the quantity and

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:03.880
quality of the arms provided [to] Taiwan [should]
be conditioned entirely on the threat posed

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.079
by the PRC.”

00:10:06.079 --> 00:10:10.880
In other words: the U.S. hasn’t written
Taiwan a blank check to militarize.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:16.390
It sells exactly what’s required to maintain
a comfortable balance of power – nothing

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:18.270
more, nothing less.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:24.990
And, in the last decade or so, the balance
has shifted unmistakably in China’s favor.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:30.200
First, America has successfully stigmatized
independence in Taiwan.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:34.769
No prominent politician has advocated for
it in years.

00:10:34.769 --> 00:10:39.650
And second, not only has the size and strength
of the Chinese military grown, but the party

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:44.250
has taken a more aggressive tone under Xi
Jinping.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:48.580
Add to that the Russian invasion of Ukraine
and you can see why Biden might feel the need

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:52.120
to act decisively to restore the balance.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:58.560
So, in the most straightforward sense, what
he said was new and significant.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:03.430
In the broader context of America’s attempts
to maintain a balance, he was only re-calibrating

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:07.990
a policy unchanged since 1979.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:13.230
One thing to keep in mind is that “strategic
ambiguity” has never been official U.S.

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:14.279
policy.

00:11:14.279 --> 00:11:19.400
This term has always been an unofficial description
of how journalists and academics perceive

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:21.550
U.S. policy.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:27.380
The Taiwan Relations Act doesn’t state “We
may or may not defend Taiwan”, it just…

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:29.560
doesn’t say anything.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:36.829
Besides, ambiguity and clarity aren’t binary
options, they’re opposite ends of a spectrum.

00:11:36.829 --> 00:11:40.650
The answer to a question on “60 Minutes”
is less credible than a comment made during

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:46.140
an official speech, which is less credible
than a commitment formalized in a treaty.

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:50.340
Ultimately, the question “Would the U.S.
Defend Taiwan?”

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:53.820
can only be answered by the sitting president.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:59.410
And because the particular location and circumstances
matter, Biden was effectively speculating

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:03.870
out loud about his own anticipated reaction.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:08.339
Anyone who claims he’s shifted to a policy
of complete clarity is either overlooking

00:12:08.339 --> 00:12:12.459
or presuming the answer to difficult questions.

00:12:12.459 --> 00:12:16.339
Questions like: how would he respond to an
invasion only of Kinmen?

00:12:16.339 --> 00:12:21.670
It and other so-called ‘indefensible’
outlying islands were specifically excluded

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:24.620
from its former defense treaty.

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:29.490
Hypotheticals like these may sound tedious
and trivial, but it’s exactly these red

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:34.860
lines – or their absence – that determine
a commitment’s credibility.

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:39.889
To summarize: Biden has no doubt moved in
the direction of clarity but there remain

00:12:39.889 --> 00:12:42.149
plenty of uncertainties.

00:12:42.149 --> 00:12:44.930
But… hold on.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:51.750
More important than what Biden said, how he
meant it, or why, is what effect it has.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:56.210
We don’t need to assess anyone’s intentions
or debate whether this represents a change

00:12:56.210 --> 00:13:00.880
in U.S. policy to determine whether these
actions increased or decreased the likelihood

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:02.279
of conflict.

00:13:02.279 --> 00:13:07.490
So self-obsessed is the world of U.S. foreign
policy that it’s easy to forget something

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:13.820
extremely basic: China already assumes it
would defend Taiwan.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:18.949
Military strategy 101 dictates that the People’s
Liberation Army must expect and prepare for

00:13:18.949 --> 00:13:21.230
American intervention.

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:23.690
Anything else would be dangerously reckless.

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:29.070
And, indeed, many of the weapons Beijing has
invested most heavily in are aimed at, in

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:33.250
the words of the Pentagon, “denying third-party
intervention”.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:37.480
You can probably guess the identity of that
“third-party”.

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:41.750
In other words, it’s very unlikely that
a verbal commitment from Biden would fundamentally

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:44.550
alter Beijing’s calculus.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:50.200
Whatever it planned to do before, it probably
still plans to do now.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:54.209
But it’s not so much the content of the
president’s comments that likely worries

00:13:54.209 --> 00:13:56.660
China, it’s their tone.

00:13:56.660 --> 00:14:01.370
Tellingly, the Chinese Foreign Ministry’s
reaction was to warn against sending the,

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:06.150
quote, “wrong signals to the separatist
forces of Taiwan independence”.

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:13.329
To translate: it was the implication that
concerned China, not so much the words themselves.

00:14:13.329 --> 00:14:17.420
Since the very beginning of the two countries’
relationship, China has been deeply suspicious

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:22.149
that America is only using Taiwan as a way
to undermine it.

00:14:22.149 --> 00:14:26.149
Armed with this underlying distrust, it’s
extremely sensitive to any indication that

00:14:26.149 --> 00:14:31.449
America is abandoning the status quo and moving
in the direction of independence.

00:14:31.449 --> 00:14:38.270
Ironically, the last 44 years of peace prove
that all three parties want the same thing.

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:41.310
At least, they’re willing to tolerate the
same thing.

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:47.140
But Biden’s comments inadvertently feed
into Beijing’s suspicions.

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:51.750
Fearing this is the beginning of a trend toward
independence, China tries to credibly demonstrate

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:54.959
its concern by reacting assertively.

00:14:54.959 --> 00:14:59.990
The U.S. then interprets these often quite
dangerous moves as a challenge to the peaceful

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:06.269
equilibrium and acts to offset the change,
which again prompts China to react.

00:15:06.269 --> 00:15:11.700
It’s this spiral of escalating reactions
that most worries experts.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:18.060
It’s how both countries could veer into
a conflict that neither one wants.

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:22.649
As Cornell Professor Jessica Chen Weiss has
said, the challenge is figuring out how to

00:15:22.649 --> 00:15:27.750
deter China without provoking it into action
or validating its fears.

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:33.250
Fortunately, we’re not stuck between the
choice of either capitulating to China or

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:36.620
needlessly escalating tensions.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:41.730
For over four decades, America has sold Taiwan
billions of dollars worth of its best weapons

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:46.839
– proving that it can maintain peace without
inciting war.

00:15:46.839 --> 00:15:52.350
Remember, the time-tested approach is to ensure
that (a) tomorrow perpetually feels like a

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:58.000
better time to act drastically than today,
and (b) both countries are reassured that

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:01.670
their worst case remains improbable.

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:06.180
One way to accomplish the former is to increase
the cost of an attack by investing in the

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.019
right defenses.

00:16:08.019 --> 00:16:14.029
Currently, Taiwan is incentivized to buy a
few expensive and flashy things like submarines

00:16:14.029 --> 00:16:16.130
and fighter jets.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:22.390
Purchases like these help boost morale and
project an image of toughness to voters.

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:27.330
But experts consistently argue a more effective
approach would involve a “large number of

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:28.730
little things”.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:34.569
This strategy, sometimes called a “porcupine”
or “overall” defense concept, could include

00:16:34.569 --> 00:16:41.570
laying mines, building anti-ship defenses,
or stockpiling resources in case of a blockade.

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:47.180
None of these actions would cross any of China’s
red lines – the U.S. already sells weapons

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:51.540
– but they would help strengthen Taiwan’s
defenses.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:57.649
Now, there is another elephant in the room
we should address: Biden’s recent comments

00:16:57.649 --> 00:17:03.959
are representative of an unspoken, bipartisan
consensus: that a Chinese invasion is inevitable

00:17:03.959 --> 00:17:05.959
– or even imminent.

00:17:05.959 --> 00:17:11.790
“If” has slowly become “when” – a
mindset that encourages quick and sometimes

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:14.200
reckless action.

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:17.040
Reality is much more complicated.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:20.699
Much of the “evidence” supporting this
belief is the result of misunderstandings

00:17:20.699 --> 00:17:24.419
or mis-translations of Chinese speeches.

00:17:24.419 --> 00:17:29.510
Only China would initiate a war, and therefore,
only by uncovering the specific forces at

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:35.140
play in China can you truly understand the
probability of conflict – which is why I’ve

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:38.540
made this video doing just that.

00:17:38.540 --> 00:17:44.130
In it, we take a closer look at all the evidence
for and against a near-term invasion to get

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:46.990
to the bottom of China’s timeline.

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:50.160
It won’t be available on YouTube until August.

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:55.440
But, you can watch it right now on Nebula,
our independent streaming platform where you

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:59.710
can also find my 6-part exclusive series “China,
Actually”.

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:04.710
There, we explore topics like how Chinese
censorship is both more and less pervasive

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:09.350
than we often imagine and why the country
came to dominate the world of the rare earth

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:12.929
metals ubiquitous in modern technology.

